## ⚠ Enforcement asymmetry — which guards bind you, and when
Your lane's registration is `.codex/hooks.json` (Claude Code's is `.claude/settings.json`; the hook
files in `.codex/hooks/` and `.claude/hooks/` are byte-identical). On `apply_patch` it registers
**three fail-CLOSED GUARDS** — cross-repo writes, lane-authoring, brief-rung — and **two SENSORS** (sweep-owed,
mutation-owed) that print and never deny. On Bash it registers **one more SENSOR**, the gate ladder,
which surfaces the declared tier's ladder and never denies. The exact
`mcp__codex_app__send_message_to_thread` matcher also registers brief-rung, which allows that send
(Architect-to-PM chat is not screened). On Stop it registers
one more SENSOR, `sensor-stop-notice` (blocks once, fails open). That is the whole Codex registration: the
context-pressure sensor and the Owner-comms and token-ledger Stop sensors are Claude-lane only. **In the Codex
lane even these are INERT until a human grants Codex hook trust in an interactive session, and an
untrusted hook is skipped SILENTLY**, so
never assume they are live: `node scripts/check-codex-hooks-armed.mjs` checks only the
`apply_patch` registration. They also bind write TOOLS — a file you change with a plain SHELL command is invisible to them,
a main road in the Codex lane. A human with an editor loads none of them.
**So what binds you unconditionally is THIS prose, the PM-disposition emission `core/WORKFLOW.md`
§ Gate requires after each verdict, and the `.githooks/pre-commit` hook** (`core.hooksPath=.githooks`),
which runs at commit regardless of harness and blocks a commit that has no valid lane declaration.
It checks the DECLARATION, not your task scope — no control carries a per-file allowlist, so
staying inside your task's scope binds you on your honor alone (§ Multi-writer checkout).
Full model: the kit's `PORTABILITY.md` (workflow-kit repository; not installed here).

## Multi-writer checkout — surgical staging only
If more than one agent lane writes this checkout, these rules bind **EVERY** agent. **The canonical
text is `core/MULTI_AGENT.md` § Multi-writer checkout** — the hard prohibitions are repeated here because
violating one is unrecoverable, but that section governs.

1. **NEVER blanket-stage:** no `git add -A`, `git add .`, or `git commit -a`. Stage only explicit paths
   your task touched.
2. **Verify before every commit:** run `git status`; every staged file must belong to YOUR task.
   Unexplained dirty files are the other lane's in-flight work — never stage, stash, revert, or edit
   them. If YOUR task needs a file the other lane has dirtied, STOP and ask the Owner.
3. **Substantial concurrent work → a private worktree** under a root this repo declares
   (`.claude/kit.config.json` `worktreeRoots`; `/tmp` by default) — never inside the shared checkout.
   Commit early there: only commits survive a reboot.
4. **Merge + test in the worktree, never in the shared checkout:** merge the shared branch INTO your
   worktree, install deps there (**`npm ci`** if the lockfile changed), run the **full `npm test`**
   THERE, and only then land the merge.
5. **Dependency changes are single-lane.** A lockfile CONFLICT → keep it and run `npm install`;
   never hand-merge or delete-and-regenerate.
6. **Pushing `{{DEPLOY_BRANCH}}` {{DEPLOY_EFFECT}}.** Every remote push or publication needs a fresh Owner
   GO for the exact head and target, regardless of tier or file type. A push ships the WHOLE
   branch: before any push, check `git log {{DEPLOY_REMOTE_REF}}..HEAD` for ungated work from ANY lane.

